.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt WHERE-AM-I 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm where-am-i
.Nd name the place this machine is at, from the Wi-Fi it is on
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl qv
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Fl i Ar interface
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints the name of the place this machine is at, found from the SSID
of the Wi-Fi network it is associated with: home, work, whatever the
configuration calls it, or
.Ql unknown .
.Pp
The SSID is that of the first wireless interface that is associated:
on
.Fx
the members of the
.Ql wlan
interface group, as
.Xr ifconfig 8
reports them; on Linux the interfaces with a
.Pa wireless
directory under
.Pa /sys/class/net ,
asked with
.Xr iw 8 ,
else with
.Xr wpa_cli 8 .
An SSID is trusted only while the interface is associated: a known
network out of range is not a place.
.Pp
The configuration has one line per network, the place first, then the
SSID, which is the rest of the line, spaces included but trailing
blanks dropped:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# place  ssid
home     rezo_zero
work     eduroam
.Ed
.Pp
Blank lines, and lines whose first non-blank character is
.Ql # ,
are ignored.
The first line naming the SSID decides.
Without a configuration, every place is unknown.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-i interface"
.It Fl c Ar file
Read the configuration from
.Ar file .
.It Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl i Ar interface
Ask this wireless interface alone.
.It Fl q
Quiet: print nothing, and answer with the exit status only.
.It Fl v
Say on stderr what the answer rests on: each interface asked, the SSID
it is associated with and the place it gives, whether a configuration
was found, and the wired facts: the interface the default route leaves
by, its IPv4 address, and, for a
.Xr lagg 4
on
.Fx
or a bond on Linux, its active port.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width XDG_CONFIG_HOME
.It Ev SYSFS
The root of sysfs on Linux, instead of
.Pa /sys .
.It Ev WHERE_AM_I_CONFIG
The configuration file, when
.Fl c
is not given.
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The directory of the default configuration, instead of
.Pa ~/.config .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/where-am-i
The configuration, when neither
.Fl c
nor
.Ev WHERE_AM_I_CONFIG
names one.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The place is known.
.It 1
The place is unknown, or the platform is neither
.Fx
nor Linux, in which case
.Ql unknown
is printed all the same.
.It 2
A usage error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The place, for a status bar:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ where-am-i
home
.Ed
.Pp
Do something only at work:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ [ "$(where-am-i)" = work ] && mount /net/work
.Ed
.Pp
Why the answer is what it is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ where-am-i -v -i wlan0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ifconfig 8 ,
.Xr iw 8 ,
.Xr wpa_cli 8
.Sh CAVEATS
A place is known by its SSID alone, and an SSID is not unique: any
network named like the one at home is home.
.Pp
The wired facts
.Fl v
reports are gathered for rules not yet written; no answer rests on
them.
